[00:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'DEF CON is one of the largest and longest running hacker conferences in the world. It\'s held every August in Las Vegas since 1993 and it\'s a gathering of security researchers, [music] hackers, tinkerers, journalists, curious weirdos,'

[00:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'and quite a few feds who all share an obsession [music] with taking things apart and understanding how they really work. It\'s part conference and [music] part underground bizarre with dozens of'

[00:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'villages dedicated to everything from lock picking to car hacking to IoT to social engineering [music] plus talks, competitions, badge hacking, and a whole lot of soldering irons. I go'

[00:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'to DEF CON every year because I think the people there are doing really cool and awesome stuff &gt;&gt; [music] &gt;&gt; and this year was no different. The Ludlow Institute hosted a hackathon at IoT Village where we had people hack'

[00:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'into IoT devices like [music] electronic basketball hoops, electric cars, smart lights, and other everyday connected junk and we gave out prizes when [music] people found anything interesting.'

[01:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; We\'re at IoT Village, the hardware hacking competition, uh where we are encouraging everybody to come out and hack our smart basketball court, our RC cars. So, you actually use'

[01:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a Bus Pirate to connect to the actual device to communicate with it, see what type of data is going across. It has a display. It has a web interface as well. Um you can use it to scan Bluetooth and'

[01:26] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'wireless networks. So, it\'s pretty much like the all-in-one device. &gt;&gt; I also gave a presentation at DEF CON about the pervasive hidden surveillance that\'s been quietly integrated into our lives and how most people have no idea'

[01:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'that it\'s even there. And I met some super cool people building tools to fight back. I recorded a handful of super quick interviews with some of the people I met just to give you a taste of'

[01:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'the kinds of engagement going on right now to push back against the destruction of [music] privacy in society. If you\'ve ever felt like the surveillance state is too big and too invisible to do anything about, these people are quietly proving'

[02:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'otherwise. &gt;&gt; My name is Kenneth Payne. I make hardware that detects surveillance technologies. &gt;&gt; I have one of your little tools. It\'s a flock detector. Walk me through how it works.'

[02:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; We\'re using a promiscuous Wi-Fi on an ESP32 to get things from the Wi-Fi probes of the flock cameras so that we can passively detect them without touching them. It\'s got a buzzer on it. So, if you\'re driving by one, you can kind of'

[02:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'get a beep. It\'s really good for detecting flock cams or other surveillance. So, we have essentially two PCBs, a ESP32S3 and a buzzer. It\'s a tiny little computer on here. It\'s got a little bit'

[02:42] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'of memory. And it\'s just programmed with my firmware that tells it to beep when it detects a certain It\'s an OUI. That is the first six characters of a uh Bluetooth MAC'

[02:54] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'address. So, it tells what who the company is. Now, because of uh MAC address randomization, it\'s harder to just track by OUIs on certain devices. So, now we\'re starting to look at uh some of the Bluetooth UUIDs and some of the the deeper parts of Bluetooth that'

[03:07] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'you can still passively detect. They Of course, now people are doing this. [music] So, it\'s it\'s really relying on a lot of people who are actually actively going out and doing the research and then sharing that data that you can then use to figure out like what'

[03:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'what is What are they doing with this next and how can we then alter our detection methods? &gt;&gt; And so, you\'re saying that we need more researchers [music] out there. We need people actually keeping up with this. And uh what kind of work can they be doing?'

[03:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; You could get wiggle on your phone or even start to just look up war driving and you you\'ll you\'ll be really surprised at how much uh surveillance you see. That\'s I didn\'t even I wasn\'t aware of how much surveillance there was until I started war driving. And then I'

[03:46] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'noticed that it\'s just everywhere, you know? And so, it I would it was alarming to me. And I\'m like, "Wow, it would be interesting if we just made something that detected all this easily for the average person." If you want to get get a hold of one of these, you can go to'

[03:58] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'kernelpanic.tech. I even sell the PCBs for cheaper or if you really want to do this, you don\'t even need the device. You can just buy this component and this component and you can easily see on my art where the wiring is and you can just wire it up'

[04:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'yourself and it\'s going to cost you less than $10. &gt;&gt; It\'s such a cool tiny little gadget that could be so powerful for helping people protect themselves. I\'m going to be plugging this in and I\'m going to go detect me some plug cameras.'

[04:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Awesome. &gt;&gt; That thanks so much. &gt;&gt; I am Cooper Quintin aka Cybertiger. I am a senior staff technologist at EFF where I work on a number of things including Ray Hunter, the open-source IMSI-catcher'

[04:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'detecting hardware and software. Ray Hunter is open-source software which runs on a mobile hotspot that you can buy for $20 to $40 on eBay and Amazon. And so we\'ve been able to'

[04:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'write software that takes a look at the control traffic going on between your modem and the tower and looks at what signals the cell phone tower is sending you. And it looks for signatures or signs of a stingray. [music] A stingray'

[05:01] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is a tool that\'s used by police which is a fake cell tower and it tricks your phone into connecting to it so that it can find your identity, your IMSI which is your unique identifier for your SIM'

[05:13] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'card. Once it finds that, it can be used by law enforcement to locate you. It can be used by law enforcement or criminals [music] or spies to listen to your phone calls and text messages or it can be'

[05:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'used by scammers, spammers, or anyone [music] else. So it\'s really easy for someone to set up a fake cell tower and say, "All cell phones in the area, please connect to this tower. It\'s'

[05:39] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'very important." And so all the phones will do that. So we built Ray Hunter to try to get a better idea of how often [music] this tech was being used in the US, how prevalent it was, and [music]'

[05:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'how exactly it works because there\'s been a lot of um obfuscation of what [music] exactly the attacks are that that are taking place and how we can better secure our phones against this'

[06:05] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'technology. [music] One of our big goals was to make counter surveillance device that was cheap, accessible, and easy to run not just for hackers, but for everyone. You can go to eff.org/rayhunter.'

[06:17] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'So, once you have the hardware, you can plug it into your computer with a USB cable or connect it over to our Wi-Fi. You can download our software and there\'s an installer there you can run. Get this little device here. It\'ll have'

[06:29] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'a little green line at the top. If that line turns red, that means that Ray Hunter has found something it considers suspicious. Um and then you can connect to it, download the Pcaps. So, a Pcap is a network traffic [music]'

[06:43] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'packet capture that you can view in programs like Wireshark. You can download the file, send it to us, and we\'ll take a look at it and tell you what we think. &gt;&gt; This is the Biscuit Ultra. It\'s designed by a guy named Hedge. It\'s used for war'

[06:55] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'driving. It also war drives for flock cameras, Bluetooth devices, and it has a counter [music] anti-surveillance feature. First, it records all devices in the vicinity that it can find, and if it detects the same'

[07:08] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'device within [music] a certain interval, then it alerts you that you may be uh having a tail and save it to an SD card [music] for further analysis. You could scan for APs. This Wi-Fi only war drive. You have Bluetooth [music] and flock. Everything or'

[07:21] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'anti-surveillance mode. And checks for any Axon security cameras in the area and puts them in the map [music] where it detects that they\'re located. &gt;&gt; Where can someone purchase this? &gt;&gt; Google Biscuit Ultra or Biscuit [music]'

[07:33] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'Pro. He makes two versions. &gt;&gt; What is this device? &gt;&gt; This is a LilyGo T-Deck. And in here I installed the Def [music] Con 34 Meshtastic, which is a Laura, but it\'s configured for the different channels'

[07:47] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] for Def Con. They have a web flasher that you use. &gt;&gt; And so, you just flash new software on there. Is that the idea? Oh, cool. &gt;&gt; So, once [music] you flash it, you could enable Bluetooth. There\'s a Meshtastic app, and then you pair it with your'

[07:59] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'device. Then you could use your phone to type and receive [music] and send and receive messages. This is another Meshtastic Laura device. These are two separate devices. They pair to both of [music] my phones. So, I could either'

[08:11] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'control the channels with my phone or with a device. So, you [music] would see the conversation on your phone like this. It\'s cool because it\'s off-grid, meaning that it\'s a mesh network [music] operating around 915 MHz in the US, and'

[08:24] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] it goes from node to node, meaning that this device goes to the nearest device it sees and speak even across two different hotels and different areas.'

[08:36] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; New to the kinds of shenanigans at Def Con. This is a backpack with a portable 3D printer. Beautiful. &gt;&gt; It also looks for devices that follow you. So, like if somebody put like an'

[08:48] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'AirTag or something, it looks for like signal strength and time. &gt;&gt; Sweet. &gt;&gt; Also, will look for like Bluetooth and Wi-Fi probe requests for Flock cameras as well. Oh, hell yeah.'

[09:00] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'You can click on them and it gives more information about like each each tracker. It\'ll also sniff for probe requests to see what your weak pleasure is.'

[09:12] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '&gt;&gt; Oh, sweet. &gt;&gt; So, like you can put your phone by it and then to see what it\'s putting out. &gt;&gt; All your networks that you\'ve been on. &gt;&gt; My main takeaway from this year\'s Def Con is that no one is coming [music] to save us. If we want to understand what'

[09:25] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'is going on in our tech, we have to break it open ourselves. The cell networks aren\'t going to get more secure because a carrier decides [music] to be nice. And Flock isn\'t going to stop blanketing our neighborhoods with'

[09:38] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'cameras [music] because a community asked politely. The tools that protect us are being built by a small, scrappy community of hackers and hobbyists [music] working on shoestring budgets, often in their spare'

[09:51] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'time. Tools like a $20 hotspot flashed with Rey Hunter and a $10 ESP32 that beeps when you drive [music] past a Flock camera. And a pager-sized gadget that warns you when the same car has'

[10:03] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'been following you for too long. I love seeing this stuff. [music] And if you have anything that you\'ve prototyped, come show me at a conference sometime. The surveillance in our lives is pervasive, [music]'

[10:15] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'but it\'s also detectable. And the more of us who are actually looking, the more empowered [music] we can all be to protect ourselves in the digital age. Ludlow Institute is a nonprofit research [music] and media institute focused on'

[10:27] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: 'advancing freedom through technology. We teach you how to use tech that works for you, not against you. If you\'d like to support our efforts, visit ludlowinstitute.org/donate [music] or simply just share some of our content'

[10:40] [scene:talk]
VISUAL: 
AUDIO: '[music] and spread the word. We definitely need more people in this fight. And check out the t-shirt designs in our merch shop. I saw Afro Man recently and he was a big fan of this one.'
